ow this is mutualistic so  the reason this is a mutualistic  relationship is the bacteria supply  ammonia that the plant desperately needs  the plants roots will take in the  ammonia and grow what about the bacteria  the bacteria benefits because it will  the plant supplies nutrients  particularly produced through  photosynthesis that the bacteria needs  to survive so it's a great example of a  mutualistic relationship with plants  excuse me with plants and bacteria so  kind of a more classic example of  mutualism involving plants is a  relationship between plants and  pollinating animals in this case the  plant supplies food or nectar for  whoever the pollinator is whether it's  bumble bee whether it's hummingbird and  in the picture here you see it's a  butterfly so the plant supplies food  what about the pollinator the pollinator  spreads pollen for the plant as the  pollinator flies from flower to flower  to flower it spreads pollen and achieves  pollination in order for the plant to  reproduce so again a classic example of  mutualism well let's not forget that  plants have to defend themselves against  herbivore predators and plants have all  kinds of adaptations to defend  themselves against herbivores  spines thorns chemicals to fight off  herbivores you know in the picture here  here's a cactus and you can see all the  thorns that surround the  yes and if you're a hungry herbivore  you're probably going to have a hard  time eating this plant and so you'll  choose to eat another plant that is less  protected so I've got a wonderful  adaptation in order to defend themselves  against herbivores you know I grew up in  Illinois and I had to be very wary of  poison ivy forever playing baseball or  throwing a frisbee around and the ball  or the frisbee lands in a patch of  poison ivy you have to know what it  looks like and the same if you haven't  heard it before is called leaves of  three let it be here we see the the  telltale signal of poison ivy these  three leaves grouped together and if you  ever do reach in to get the baseball and  get the frisbee well you might come down  with with a real nasty rash couple days  later so really itchy it's not  life-threatening but my point is that  poison ivy is is is poisonous again not  poisonous the point where a person is  going to die but herbivores become weary  of eating plants that get them sick or  that could give them a really nasty rash  and so it's a great defense mechanism  against herbivores so there you go  there's the end of this video  you know if you're in my biology class  pause the video try to answer these  questions and I'd be happy to check your  answers before school or after school  one day so go ahead and pause the video  and good luck
 

